FH6 runtime: pool stub slots, so rewritten stubs stop growing the code buffer

Applies on top of 0001-0004. Not part of bin/libarm64ecfex.dll yet.

The game keeps about half a million one-instruction stubs (an instruction
and a jmp) in anonymous memory and rewrites them about 2 million times a
minute. Each rewrite fails the stub's SMC check, and the next run of the
stub loaded a fresh copy, from the disk cache or as a pool clone, into new
code buffer memory that was never freed. In the world that is about 1 GB a
minute, until Android kills the game.

- Give each stub entry up to three slots, carved from 8 MB arenas in the
  code buffer, and write a new copy over the oldest slot with the same
  entry offset. Drop the entry's stash entry when its slot is reused.
- Never link an exit inside an arena, so no link record points into a
  slot.
- Send pool clones and single-entry disk-cache loads of anonymous code
  through the slots.
- Clear the stash and the slots in GuestToHostMap::ClearCache.
- FH6Stats: PoolSlotReuse, PoolSlotNew, PoolSlotFallback.

